member.summary:
A time budget — and, optionally, a cancellation token — that covers a whole host operation instead of a
single entry into the engine.


Every public entry that runs script (Execute, Evaluate, Invoke, Engine.Call,
the JsValue.Call extension helpers) is a complete top-level run: the engine resets every
registered constraint before the entry and again on the way out. That is what makes a reused engine
usable, but it also means  arms a
fresh deadline for each entry. An operation built from several entries — import a module, call
the component it exported, then invoke a handler once per row — therefore has no wall-clock bound at
all, however small the configured interval: foreach (var row in rows) handler.Call(row); gives
every row the whole interval to itself. This constraint is the in-box answer for that shape. The host
brackets the operation, and nothing the engine does in between rewinds the budget.



// one instance per engine, owned by the host
var deadline = new OperationDeadlineConstraint();
var engine = new Engine(options => options.Constraint(deadline));

deadline.Begin(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2), cancellationToken);
try
{
    var component = engine.Modules.Import("./app.js");
    foreach (var row in rows)
    {
        engine.Invoke("render", row);
    }
}
finally
{
    deadline.End();
}




The instance is host-owned on purpose: Begin and End are not something the engine can
infer, since only the host knows where its operation starts and stops. Register it with
 and keep the reference, or — when one
 is shared by several engines — register a factory
() and reach each engine's
own instance through engine.Constraints.Find<OperationDeadlineConstraint>().



What it does not do. It is not a cross-engine budget: one instance carries one deadline and one
token, and an engine is single-threaded by contract, so the instance expects the same thread discipline
as the engine it is registered with — Begin, the entries it covers, and End all happen on
the thread driving that engine. It bounds nothing while disarmed, which is deliberate and close to
free: outside a / bracket a check reads two fields and takes no
timestamp, so an engine that is pooled between operations pays nothing for carrying it.

remarks.para:
The motivating case is a one-line adoption assertion. Those factories fall back to the per-object
dictionary silently and correctly when they cannot shape an object, and two of the triggers are not
knowable at the call site, so the only way to know the optimization was actually obtained is to ask:

var row = JsObject.CreateFromEntries(engine, fields);
Assert.True(engine.Advanced.HasSharedShape(row));

member.example:
The per-request shape the  registration cannot express, since the values are
only known once the request is in hand:

engine.Advanced.AddLazyGlobal("user", _ => JsValue.FromObject(engine, request.User));
engine.Advanced.AddLazyGlobal("db", e => new ObjectWrapper(e, scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IDb>()));

Neither the user projection nor the database wrapper is built for a script that never mentions
the name.

The shape the options-time registration cannot otherwise express — a process-wide
 whose factories capture nothing, with the per-request part supplied per engine:

// once per process
var options = new Options().AddLazyGlobal("db", static e =>
    JsValue.FromObject(e, ((IServiceProvider) e.Advanced.HostDefined!).GetRequiredService<IDb>()));

// once per request
var engine = new Engine(options);
engine.Advanced.HostDefined = scope.ServiceProvider;

The ordering works because the factory is lazy: it runs on the first script read of db, which
is necessarily after the constructor returned and the state was attached. An
 callback runs during construction, and can set
this but must not expect a later request's value to be visible to it.


The engine stores the value verbatim, so the natural retrieval is a type pattern:
if (engine.Advanced.HostDefined is RequestContext ctx).

The reuse shape: capture once at the end of construction, keep the snapshot in a field beside the
engine, and restore in a finally so an evaluation that throws cannot leave its globals
behind for the next one.

private readonly object _gate = new();
private readonly Engine _engine;
private readonly GlobalSnapshot _clean;

public ScriptRunner(object host)
{
    _engine = new Engine();
    _engine.SetValue("host", host);                  // all configuration first
    _clean = _engine.Advanced.CaptureGlobalSnapshot();
}

public object? Run(string script)
{
    lock (_gate)                                     // an Engine is single-threaded
    {
        try
        {
            return _engine.Evaluate(script).ToObject();
        }
        finally
        {
            _engine.Advanced.RestoreGlobalSnapshot(_clean);
        }
    }
}

The finally is the point: a script that throws still declared its globals, and restoring
only on the success path hands them to the next caller —  is that
try/finally in one call, for a host that would rather not spell it out. The
ToObject() is deliberate too —
returning CLR values means nothing the caller keeps depends on the engine still being in the state
that produced it. With the asynchronous entry points
(EvaluateAsync/ExecuteAsync/InvokeAsync) await the returned Task before
restoring; a restore attempted while one is outstanding throws
 rather than silently restoring underneath it.

example.code:
private static readonly JsObjectLayout PointLayout = new("x", "y", "label");

JsObject ToJs(Engine engine, Point p) => JsObject.Create(
    engine,
    PointLayout,
    [JsNumber.Create(p.X), JsNumber.Create(p.Y), new JsString(p.Label)]);

private static readonly JsObjectLayout EnvelopeLayout = JsObjectLayout.CreateBuilder()
    .Add("id")
    .Add("type")
    .AddLazy("body", static (_, state) => ((Payload) state!).ParseBody())
    .Build();

JsObject ToJs(Engine engine, Payload p) => JsObject.Create(
    engine,
    EnvelopeLayout,
    [new JsString(p.Id), new JsString(p.Type), null],
    p);

private static readonly JsObjectShape NodeShape = new JsObjectShape.Builder()
    .Method("appendChild", static (t, args) => Dom.AppendChild(t, args), length: 1)
    .Accessor("firstChild", getter: static (t, _) => Dom.GetFirstChild(t))
    .Constant("ELEMENT_NODE", 1)
    .PerRealmSlot("constructor", static o => HostRealmState.For(o.Engine).NodeConstructor, enumerable: false)
    .ToStringTag("Node")
    .Build();

// once per engine, cached by the host:
var nodeProto = NodeShape.Instantiate(engine, eventTargetProto);

static JsValue Describe(JsValue thisObject, JsValue[] arguments)
{
    for (var o = (thisObject as ObjectInstance)?.Prototype; o is not null; o = o.Prototype)
    {
        if (JsObjectShape.GetHostState(o) is NodeTypeInfo info)
        {
            return new JsString(info.InterfaceName);
        }
    }

    return JsValue.Undefined;
}

member.summary:
atanh(x), ported from msun's e_atanh.c.

  for x >= 0.5   atanh(x) = 0.5 * log1p(2 * x/(1-x))
  for x <  0.5   atanh(x) = 0.5 * log1p(2x + 2x*x/(1-x))

ECMA-262 21.3.2.7 wants NaN for NaN and for |x|>1, ±∞ for ±1 and x itself for ±0; the
three leading branches cover all four.

member.remarks:
A member that resolves purely to registered extension methods (see )
already defers to a same-named Array.prototype method on indexed array-like wrappers by default;
this option additionally makes real CLR instance methods defer.

member.example:
Deferring an expensive host object — here a CLR type projection, which otherwise builds an engine-affine
TypeReference (and resolves its members) at engine-construction time whether or not the script
mentions it:

options.AddLazyGlobal("DateTime", static engine => TypeReference.CreateTypeReference<DateTime>(engine));

// A delegate global, deferred and given a real name: DelegateWrapper always reports
// fn.name === "delegate", while a ClrFunction carries the name it was constructed with.
options.AddLazyGlobal(
    "log",
    engine => new ClrFunction(engine, "log", (_, args) => { Console.WriteLine(args.At(0)); return JsValue.Undefined; }),
    PropertyFlag.NonEnumerable);

